<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dodona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dodona in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, was the oldest oracle of the Greek world, sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dione">Dione</a>. Priests interpreted the rustling of a sacred oak, and enquirers wrote their questions on lead tablets, many of which survive. Under the Molossian kings it gained a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and became the religious centre of Epirus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dodona.jpg" alt="Dodona" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcus Cyron · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dodona-Greece-April-2008-107.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dodona lay in a remote region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> away from the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>, yet it was considered second only to Delphi in prestige. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> placed Hellas around Dodona and the river Achelous and said that its inhabitants, the Selloi, had once been called Graikoi and were now Hellenes. Some scholars therefore trace the words Hellenes and Hellas to this district. The oracle was first controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotians">Thesprotians</a> and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> names Mount Tomaros as the mountain at whose foot the temple stood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Offerings of the fourteenth and thirteenth centuries BCE show that cult activity already existed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, and evidence for the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> dates from about the same time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> pray to Zeus of Dodona, and mentions priests called Selloi who slept on the ground with unwashed feet. Some scholars hold that the oracle was originally that of a Mother Goddess, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dione">Dione</a>, who shared it with Zeus.</p><p>Priestesses appear by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who gives the story of two black doves from Egyptian Thebes, one of which settled in an oak at Dodona and spoke in a human voice. Over 4,200 oracular tablets have been found, dating from the mid-sixth to the early second century BCE, all in Greek and naming more than 1,200 people, almost all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><p>About 290 BCE King Pyrrhus made Dodona the religious capital of his realm, rebuilding the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, adding a festival with games, music and drama in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and building a wall round the oracle and the holy tree. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> looted and burned the sanctuary in 219 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> rebuilt it. In 167 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under Aemilius Paulus destroyed the Molossian cities, possibly including Dodona. Emperor Julian consulted the oracle in 362 CE. Pilgrims still came until 391 or 392 CE, when Emperor Theodosius closed the pagan temples and cut down the ancient oak. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Dodona attended the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Dodona never eclipsed Delphi but its fame spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In the Argonautica of Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> the ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a> could prophesy because it contained an oak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from Dodona. Ancient writers disagreed about the oracle's origins: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a>, said the Pelasgi founded it, and reported a story that it was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. According to a recent interpretation, the sound of the oracle came from bronze objects hung from the oak branches and rung by the wind like a chime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dodona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the rise of the city-states in the 8th century BCE, sanctuaries grew: the first temples were built to house images of the gods, and great sanctuaries shared by all Greeks developed at Olympia, Delphi, Delos and <b>Dodona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The ancients themselves placed the poet early: Herodotus, having consulted the Oracle at <b>Dodona</b>, put Homer and Hesiod about 400 years before his own time, which would be around 850 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on pastoralism, with <b>Dodona's</b> oracle of Zeus as the great religious centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a></li></ul></section>
